 ],
 "literal": "Midnight has become dark indeed. Speech having subsided, / sweetly settled down indeed have people, without grief. / Even the wide-placed world sleeps. / Only I, surely, am one who does not sleep.",
 "translation": "Midnight has fallen, dark and deep. Speech has died down, and people have settled into sweet, peaceful sleep, free of grief. Even the wide world is asleep. I alone, surely, remain awake.",
 "modern": "நள்ளிரவு வந்துவிட்டது. பேச்சுக்கள் அடங்கி மக்கள் வருத்தமின்றி இனிமையாகத் தூங்கிவிட்டார்கள். அகன்ற இடத்தைக் கொண்ட இந்த உலகமும் தூங்குகிறது. நான் ஒருத்தி மட்டும்தான் நிச்சயமாகத் தூங்காமல் இருக்கிறேன்.",
 "explanation": "In this Neytal (seashore) poem, the heroine speaks to herself in the deep of the night, expressing the intense pain of separation from her lover. She observes that midnight has fallen and all human speech has ceased. People have peacefully gone to sleep, free from any worries or grief, and even the vast world itself is asleep. In stark contrast to this universal peace, she is the only one kept awake by her agonizing longing. The image of a sleeping world highlights her profound loneliness and the quiet intensity of her suffering.",
